\section{Two estimators for a continuous-state language model}
\label{sec:estimators}
\paragraph{Model and sampler.}
We use ELF-B \citep{hu2026elf}: a frozen T5 encoder \citep{raffel2020t5} maps a target sequence of $L$ tokens to embeddings $x\in\mathbb R^{L\times d}$ ($d{=}512$), a transformer $D_\theta(y,t,c)$ predicts $x$ from a noisy state $y$ at time $t$ given a condition $c$, and text is read out by a factorised categorical decode of the final prediction. Generation runs a stochastic sampler for $K$ steps. Re-indexing it by its post-noising iterates gives a Markov chain with exact diagonal Gaussian kernels,
\begin{equation}
y_k=\alpha_k\,D_\theta(y_{k-1},t_{k-1},c)+\sigma_k\,\varepsilon_k,\qquad \varepsilon_k\sim\mathcal N(0,I),
\label{eq:chain}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_k=1-\gamma h_k$, $\sigma_k=\gamma h_k c_\sigma$ and $\gamma$ sets the per-step churn (Appendix~\ref{app:derivations}). Larger $\gamma$ is better at sampling: with $K{=}64$ and $\gamma{=}56$ (retention $\alpha\approx0.125$) accuracy on Countdown is $42.2$ against $28.1$ for the paper's 64-step ODE sampler, so \emph{deployment} means $\gamma56@64$ throughout ($\gamma28@32$ for Sudoku, whose sequences are longer). The trajectory likelihood is exact and factorises over the $K\times L\times d$ coordinates, so every estimator below is well defined; nothing here needs an ELBO.

\paragraph{Score-function.}
With reward $r(y_K)\in\{0,1\}$ from a verifier and any advantage $A$ (group-relative, per-step, or per-position), the policy-gradient estimator is
\begin{equation}
\hat g_{\mathrm{SF}}=\sum_{k}A_k\,\nabla_\theta\log\mathcal N\!\big(y_k;\alpha_kD_\theta,\sigma_k^2I\big)=\sum_k A_k\,\frac{\varepsilon_k^{\top}}{\sigma_k}\,\alpha_k\frac{\partial D_\theta}{\partial\theta}.
\label{eq:sf}
\end{equation}
The score of a Gaussian transition is the noise itself, $\varepsilon_k/\sigma_k$, contracted with a Jacobian. For an AR policy the corresponding quantity is $\nabla_\theta\log\pi(a_t)=(\mathbf 1_{a_t}-p_t)^{\top}\partial z_t/\partial\theta$: bounded, attached to a categorical decision that is itself the semantic event the reward depends on, and there are only $\sim$30 of them per Countdown trajectory. In~\eqref{eq:sf} there are $K\!\cdot\! L\!\cdot\! d$ noise scalars per trajectory, $5.5\times10^{5}$ at $\gamma4@16$ and $2.4\times10^{6}$ at $\gamma56@64$. None of them individually decides a token: we measured that 86\% of positions commit to their final token at the first denoising step and never change (Appendix~\ref{app:commit}), so token identity is a property of the accumulated dynamics, not of any coordinate at any step. A reward bit is therefore correlated against $10^{4}$--$10^{5}$ times more nuisance directions than in the AR case, and the resulting signal-to-noise ratio, not the quality of the advantage, is what limits learning (Appendix~\ref{app:variance}).

\paragraph{The cap is empirical, not a tuning failure.}
Table~\ref{tab:sf_variants} lists the score-function variants we ran on Countdown from the same base. Making the advantage exact (an oracle verifier applied to every intermediate decode), dense (per step and per position on a $K\times L$ grid), sparse (last-$n$ steps only), ratio-based (per-position PPO with clip fraction in the textbook range), or critic-selected (advantages from a live, jointly trained critic whose top-1 pick is correct 100\% of the time) moves the result between $+3$ and $+5$ points at the deployment sampler. The same token-level GRPO/PPO applied to a GPT-2 \citep{radford2019gpt2} fine-tuned on the same data rises from $44.2$ to $53.9$ ($+9.7$) with unremarkable clip statistics. The estimator is not broken; it is applied to the wrong kind of action.

